av_log: send FFmpeg's log lines to the instance whose thread logged them

FFmpeg has one log callback per process. mpv installed it for the first
instance only (log_mpv_instance): every FFmpeg line went to that instance,
and once it was destroyed the callback went back to FFmpeg's default
(stderr) until no instance was left. An embedder that creates its next
player while the last one still terminates (Tortuga releases an engine on
its own thread, and termination can take seconds) got a player that never
saw an FFmpeg line, among them "http: HTTP error 404 Not Found" when it
cannot open a file, which the embedder reads to tell an expired link from
a missing one. On a Pixel 7 with two engines: the 404 line of the second
engine's load arrived on the first engine's log, and after the first one
terminated no instance logged it at all.

Keep every initialized instance, oldest first, and route each line to the
instance whose thread logged it: each mpv thread that calls into FFmpeg
(core, opener, demuxer, decoder, VO and the core's thread pool jobs) names
its instance first (mp_av_log_set_thread()). A line from any other thread
(FFmpeg's own workers, a client's thread) goes to the oldest instance
alive. The callback stays installed while any instance is, and the partial
line state (buffer, prefix) is per instance. mpv master has the same code.

